  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nLarge and complex upper level trough of low pressure is starting \nto make its way on shore. Models are still showing a complex upper\nlevel setup consisting of a large parent longwave trough that \nwill stretch along the entire west coast. Making up the trough \ntrough are up to four local maximums in vorticity, that will act \nas a focus for the development of showers and thunderstorms that \nwill last through Monday morning. The first will be a weak shallow\nshortwave that will lead the parent trough, moving through \ncentral NV Thursday afternoon as it dissipates. Friday through \nSunday, the main trough energy will push in with three remaining \nvorticity maximums. One off the coast of Washington, one off the \nsouthern California channel Islands, and a third in the middle off\nof the SF Bay area. Models have shown some consistency that this \nmiddle vorticity max will become the dominant area of lift as it \nmoves over Nevada, as the southern low now looks to become \nabsorbed by the dominant trough segment. Model timing has also \nbecome more consistent as the bulk of the precipitation looks to \nfall on Sunday, with isolated to scattered showers possible Friday\nand Saturday. Current multi-day precipitation amounts have risen \nsome more given the slower movement with about a trace to 0.45\u201d of\nrain for passes and valleys, with up to 0.50\u201d to an inch plus of \nwater for the higher passes and mountains. Snow levels will again \nstart high at 9000 feet, dropping to about 7000 feet by Sunday. \nWith a trace to 0.5\u201d possible for the higher elevation passes \npasses, and up to 1 foot of snow for the range peaks. Monday \nafternoon high pressure will build in as the trough departs \nbringing quieter weather back into the region this ridge will be \nin place at least through Thursday, as models are wanting to bring\nanother trough through Nevada for the following weekend. \nTemperatures will be near to, to slightly below normal for the \nweekend,and into next week as high will range in the upper 40s to \nupper 50s, with lows in the mid 20s to upper 30s. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for the potential for several rounds of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms starting late Thursday \nafternoon, and persisting on and off through early Monday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Monday \nafternoon through at least Tuesday of next week which will bring \nquiet, dry conditions for the region.",
